  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/cc/Avengers_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Loki, banished to the Isle of Silence, mentally reaches out to Earth to find a menace to confront Thor (and thus draw him back to Asgard), sees the Hulk, and tricks him into endangering a train so that he'll take the blame, and then be hunted down by Thor.&nbsp; Instead, the lumbering Hulk actually <i>saves</i> the train, but it still looks as if he was the one that threatened it, exactly as Loki intended.\n\nNews of the would-be wrecker reaches Rick Jones, who refuses to believe that the Hulk would attack a train.&nbsp; After reading the news stories in the paper, he contacts his Teen Brigade (formed in [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6]) to try and summon heroes with enough power to stop the Hulk -- the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; But Loki doesn't want the FF interfering, so he re-routes the radio waves toward Dr. Don Blake's office.\n\nWhat Loki does <i>not</i> anticipate is that the rerouted waves reach others as well, including Hank Pym and Tony Stark.&nbsp; Soon, Ant-Man, Wasp, and Iron Man are all heading to the \"Southwest\" (which is a <i>thousand</i> miles away from NYC, as stated on page 5).&nbsp; Hours later, the FF finally detect and respond to the Teen Brigade's call for help, but as they're currently involved in another case, they suggest that <i>others</i> might be able to help.&nbsp; At that moment, the teenagers are joined all at once by Thor, Ant-Man, Wasp and Iron Man.\n\nLoki is upset that the others are complicating things and manages to lure Thor out on his own, chasing down a mental image of the Hulk he cast.&nbsp; Thor realizes it must be Loki and heads off to Asgard to have a look.&nbsp; But that leaves the others to track down the Hulk, and sure enough, he is spotted eventually by an ant, who reports back to Ant-Man.&nbsp; They track Hulk to the circus that he's been hiding in as a performer, and there is a brief scuffle, but Hulk manages to get free and head off.\n\nMeanwhile, in Asgard, Thor requests that Odin allow him to visit Loki on his island.&nbsp; Loki has prepared many traps of course, but eventually Thor and Loki face each other.&nbsp; But Loki sets loose the \"Silent Ones\" -- enemies of the gods!&nbsp; The Trolls!&nbsp; Traps, trolls and tricks don't stop Thor, who then curiously decides to take him back to Earth rather than face Odin.&nbsp; \"There, you will find others who are waiting for you... others almost as powerful as <i>I</i>!\"\n\nBack on Earth, Iron Man is still chasing after the Hulk, who is heading toward Detroit, and they face off inside an auto-factory.&nbsp; But Thor drops in with Loki and explains how he tried to frame the Hulk to lure Thor into battle with the behemoth.&nbsp; Loki makes himself radioactive and orders Hulk and Iron Man to flee before they're killed by the radiation, allowing him and Thor to resume hostilities, but Ant-Man and Wasp arrive on the scene, and activate a trapdoor that sends Loki down into a lead-lined tank, which will protect everyone until Loki can no longer continue, giving Thor a chance to return him to where he belongs.\n\nBut before he departs, Ant-Man proposes that they form a team.&nbsp; \"If we combine forces, we could be almost unbeatable!\"&nbsp; Iron Man is all for it, and Thor agrees that they could do much good.&nbsp; Hulk is tired of being pursued, so he also agrees, and Jan proposes the name \"Avengers,\" which sticks.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Its scattered pacing and head‑scratching Hulk moments aside, the significance of watching the Avengers form is undeniable -- the spark is enough to elevate it.",
  "subtitle": "A Meandering Beginning Ignites a Real Spark",
  "review_text": "<b>Chronology Note:</b>\n[BR] Loki's presence on the so-called \"Isle of Silence\" seems to be out of step with where we saw him <i>last</i> time, in May's [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94|2026-09-29], where we found him chained to a wall on a cliff with \"uru menacles [sic:&nbsp; should be <i>manacles</i>]\" near Asgard (see page 4 of [LINK:1963:JIM_94_thor0|JIM #94|2026-09-29]).&nbsp; He's even shown to still be there on page 8 of <i>this</i> month's [LINK:1963:JIM_96_thor0|JIM #96|2026-11-28]!\n\nSo the natural question is:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Exactly <i>when</i> did this first Avengers story take place?]\n\nI <b><i>get</i></b> it -- it's a story, not to mention a very groundbreaking one, and I've stated many times that doing the chronology two-step is <i>not</i> something that Longbox Playback chooses to engage in.&nbsp; I instead plow forward in strict publication order sequence, as explained [PAGE: reading-order | here].\n\nBut while I choose not to penalize the story for the inconsistency (because of the obvious <i>intent</i>), I still am allowed to [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=scratch my head]... It creates a mild whiplash effect for anyone following the books as they hit the stands, due to the fact that Loki appears to be simultaneously imprisoned in two different places with no narrative bridge between them.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I could even go so far as to suggest that this story takes place <i>after</i> the events of JIM issue 96, but as there's no direct reference, I'm forced to stick with the reading order rule -- which states that the JIM issue was, in fact, published one day <i>after</i> this issue.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | LokisRovingEyes | width=14%] The 22-page story itself reads far more like a Thor solo tale, with everyone else simply getting swept up in the action.&nbsp; Thor and Loki, <i>without</i> the others, consume a <i>huge</i> chunk of the pages.&nbsp; Loki provides the catalyst to get everyone together, with the Hulk being the perfect foil.\n[BR]\n[BR] Incidentally, Jack Kirby does a <i>fantastic</i> job showing Loki's eyes roving over Earth -- <i>clearly</i> it's an <i>effect</i>, rather than being literal eyes, but showing them in the background of virtually every early panel is nonetheless very effective.&nbsp; This visual adds an eerie omnipresence to Loki that fits perfectly with the idea that he's manipulating events from afar.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Great job, Jack!]\n\nWhile Loki is able to re-route the radio waves <i>away</i> from the Baxter Building in order to prevent the meddlesome Fantastic Four from getting involved, what he <i>doesn't</i> realize is who <i>else</i> heard the broadcast.&nbsp; Within moments, it's <i>far more than Thor</i> who sets out to stop what appears to be an out-of-control Hulk, and the Teen Brigade is suddenly surrounded by an assortment of heroes, all of whom have come out West to see if they can help:\n[ASSET: center | OtherHeroesArrive | width=67%]\nIt's the first time the Marvel universe feels like it's folding in on itself -- vastly diverse characters from separate books suddenly sharing the same crisis.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhile Loki <i>does</i> succeed in finally luring Thor back to Asgard, what he doesn't count on is the fact that the <i>other</i> heroes are still in the Southwest and still have their sights on tracking down the Hulk, and this is the point at which the book suffers the most, as it shows the Hulk posing as the robot \"Mechano\" in a circus, hiding his true identity so as to give him time to figure out his next step.\n\n[ASSET: left | EverSpyingAnts | width=16%] And I have to say that, despite the fact that the <i>Hulk</i> magazine failed to ever establish what the Hulk did during his down time, I can't imagine <i>any</i> reader would have predicted this!&nbsp; The idea of the Hulk quietly biding his time under a circus spotlight feels wildly at odds with his usual smash-first, brood-later pattern.&nbsp; We already <i>saw</i> him earlier out rescuing the train.\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Now</i> he's wearing clown make-up?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's quite the double-take!)]\n\nNot only that, but if the Hulk was \"hiding\" under cover of the circus, what was he accomplishing on pages 2 and 3, shown leaping around the countryside?&nbsp; (And while I'm on the topic, since <i>when</i> has the Hulk ever worried about whether a train was about to crash?)\n[BR]\n[BR] All this highlights once again how poorly the Hulk has been portrayed as a character.&nbsp; His actions are random and impossible to predict, largely because Stan and Jack never bothered to establish him.&nbsp; His motivations shift from issue to issue, making it hard to tell whether he's meant to be a misunderstood monster, a tragic figure, or simply a plot device.&nbsp; Wouldn't it be far more likely for Dr. Banner to have reconnected with the US army and try to be near Betty Ross?\n\nThe fact of the matter is that Stan and Jack don't seem worried about that -- the Hulk is the issue's foil from start to finish, and thanks to the trillions of ever spying ants, Ant-Man is alerted, leading to the inevitable confrontation that consumes about 6 pages of story (pages 9-14), all while Thor is up in Asgard with the intent of confronting his evil brother.\n[ASSET: center | SixPages | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | LokiBackToEarth | width=19%] Why Thor chooses to return Loki to Earth after spending four and a half more pages of story evading traps and ultimately capturing him (as opposed to simply presenting him to Odin) -- this all makes very little sense.\n[BR]\n[BR] It reads less like a character decision and more like a narrative necessity to get Loki back into the main plot before the issue runs out of pages.&nbsp; The transition is so sudden that it almost feels we missed a panel or some helpful editorial note.\n\nBut that's exactly what's <i>happened</i>.&nbsp; During all this mayhem, Jack has managed to consume about [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=half the entire book] on little more than theater, and the actual plot that kind of ended on page 7 has now leapfrogged its way to page 19.\n\nThe next thing you know, the action resumes for <i>another</i> two pages with Iron Man in pursuit of the Hulk, who is shown leaping his way to Detroit.&nbsp; It's a chaotic, industrial backdrop that suits both characters -- metal, machinery, and raw force everywhere you look.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Who would have guessed the Avengers began life in <i>Detroit</i>, of all places?]&nbsp; Inside the auto factory, the human members of the team continue battling before Thor shows up with Loki in tow.\n\nLo and behold, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=there's only two pages left!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But... I'm betting that was the <i>goal</i>...)]\n[ASSET: center | HulkVsIronMan | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | LokiNotDone | width=18%] [ASSET: right | LeadLinedTank | width=13%] Of course, even <i>then</i>, Loki still believes he can drive away the others by making himself radioactive, leaving just himself and Thor to settle the matter between the two of them.\n[BR]\n[BR] Loki conveniently stands in the one place where Ant-Man can instruct his ants to trap him inside a lead-lined tank, giving the five heroes a final moment to speak about the possible formation of a new super-team.&nbsp; It's brief, almost abrupt, but it carries the spark of something larger -- the sense that such <i>vastly</i> mismatched figures might actually become a unit.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt's not a <i>great</i> origin tale, and as I said above, there are large chunks of it that come across as a solo Thor adventure, where the other heroes are simply along for the ride.&nbsp; That being <i>said</i>, a team must begin <i>somewhere</i>, but it seems as if the story itself probably wasn't even up to mid-grade standards (due to vast swaths of it being wasted pages of needless battle action).\n\nQuite frankly, the book feels torn between being a Thor showcase and a team debut, and the tug‑of‑war leaves the plot thinner than it should be.&nbsp; You keep <i>waiting</i> for the team to truly take center stage, but the story never quite hands them the reins.\n\nNevertheless, it still gets extra points for the thrill of seeing all the characters we've been reading about these last few months (save Spider-Man and the Fantastic Four of course) actually come together.\n\nEven with its structural wobble, the sheer significance of watching the Marvel universe widen for the first time -- the thrill of these heroes converging, the spark of a new era forming right on the page -- lifts the issue above mid‑grade.&nbsp; Spotty plotting or not, it's hard to imagine any reader not <i>immediately</i> leaning forward... [SPAN: severity=success; text=and that justifies my final rating.]\n\n[ASSET: left| WaspReactions | width=14%] The Wasp doesn't do much in this particular adventure, but I still liked Jan's commentary on all the other members (e.g. Thor being \"dreamy\" and Iron Man \"hideous\"), as she continues to try to make Hank jealous -- it's <i>completely</i> in character for her.\n[BR]\n[BR] If this is her main contribution (at least for this one issue), it does at least hint at an interpersonal flavor that will likely relax the team's chemistry going forward.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even in limited panel time, she adds a human texture the others often lack.\n\nI also liked the way that Loki, to the <i>very end</i>, treated the other heroes with [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=absolute disdain].&nbsp; They were simply in his way, <i>beneath </i>him, and even as late as page 21, he just wanted them to <b><i>be gone</i></b> so that he and Thor could get down to the serious business of beating each other's brains out, even calling the Hulk a \"human dolt!\"&nbsp; This, too, was <i>absolutely</i> in character (and I loved it).&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Not to mention the fact that it very much mimics the hilarious interaction between he and the Hulk in the first <i>Avengers</i> MCU movie.&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>]\n\nHaving seen many issues now of Jack Kirby artwork, it's clear he did the <i>full penciling</i> on this one, as everyone had that <i>unmistakable</i> Kirby look and treatment, likely leaving the inker with lighter duty this time around, as if Kirby wanted to make sure the book got off to a good start.&nbsp; (And let's be <i>honest</i>:&nbsp; from an artistic perspective, it did...)&nbsp; The consistency in the linework gave the issue a unified feel, even when the plot itself was zig-zagging.\n\nThat final panel of the five heroes together, with both Iron Man and Thor's arms raised, was especially memorable:&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And has been paid homage many times over the decades since.&nbsp; Even without knowing what comes next, the image radiates the promise of a new era, and you can't blame future creators for wanting to show it time and time again.&nbsp; It's the kind of image that feels iconic even without context -- a visual mission statement.]\n[ASSET: center | AvengersIsBorn | width=50%]\n\nIt's a decent start, and a just-above-mid-grade rating seems about right here.&nbsp; An origin story that is absolutely <i>necessary</i>, but outside of the obvious significance it has as the beginning of a new book-length series, it was otherwise surprisingly unremarkable as an <i>actual read</i>.\n\nFinally... whatever happened to the Teen Brigade?&nbsp; Did someone inform them that the Hulk has been corralled?\n[BR] They vanish from the narrative as abruptly as they entered it.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Or were we just supposed to <i>overlook</i> that little detail?)]",
  "side_note": [
    "One is also forced to wonder if Hulk's \"I pity the guy who tries to beat us!\" ultimately influenced the catch phrase \"I pity the fool...(<i>fill in the blank</i>)\" of a once-famous 1980's TV character!\n\nIt's <b><i>probably</i></b> coincidence, but it is still amusing to imagine the Hulk as an unlikely linguistic trailblazer."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "I confess my affection and bias toward the Avengers as a book, but I don't remember ever reading this story before.&nbsp; If I did, it was many decades ago, so it will be interesting starting over from the beginning like everyone else back in the Silver Age days and seeing the team morph over time.\n\nThere's something refreshing about approaching these early tales without the weight of later history -- just the raw, formative storytelling of the era -- the charm of watching these characters before they become legends -- still just figures finding their footing.",
  "highlight": "The story wanders, the Hulk confuses, but the birth of the Avengers still lands with undeniable spark."
}